<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The Short S.23 Empire flying boat's published figures, and the conditions
  each was measured in.

  From Flight: the specification panel of the standard boat in "The Aircraft
  Engineer", 29 October 1936, p. 440f; and Arthur Gouge's paper to the Royal
  Aeronautical Society on the Empire boats, 10 December 1936, as Flight printed
  it on 17 and 24 December 1936 - his Table II, "performances as obtained from
  actual flight tests", p. 680, and his account of the take-off tests at
  45,000 lb, p. 649. Read in the Internet Archive's scans; see docs/ASSETS.md.

  The standard boat's all-up weight is 40,500 lb; the long-range boats,
  Caledonia and Cambria, 45,000. No wind or state of the water is given for
  any take-off: they are flown in calm air on calm water, and held to a
  seventh of his figures - the wind and the water of a day in 1936 are worth
  that much. Gouge times a
  take-off "from the time of full throttle", which the hydraulic engine
  controls took 3 to 3 1/2 seconds to reach; the flight opens the throttles
  over 3 1/4. The run's yards are counted from the throttles' opening.

  The draught is not published. It is scaled from the general arrangement's
  side view, which draws the waterline, "W.L.": 3.9 to 4.2 ft at the main
  step by the drawn length's scale and by its height's, 88 ft and 31 ft 9 1/2
  in - taken as 4.0 ft, give or take the half foot a scaled drawing is worth.
  The drawing's waterline puts the fin's top 24 ft above the water, which is
  the "height over the water line" Flight gave on 9 July 1936. The keel is
  placed in the model's frame from the same drawing: the front view's wing
  floats' keels 4.3 ft above the hull's, and the model's floats' keels 1.71 ft
  below its hydrodynamic reference point, put the hull's keel 6.0 ft below the
  reference point; the main step is 1.82 m (5.97 ft) aft of it.

  Not flown: the stalling speed, 73 mph, at no stated weight, flap or power:
  at 40,500 lb it would take a lift coefficient of 1.98, more than the 1.8
  Gouge's wind tunnel gave with 28 degrees of flap (p. 680), which the model's,
  1.77 with its 25, matches. The cruising speed, 165 mph at 4 x 510 bhp, names
  a power and no altitude.
-->
<published_figures aircraft="short_s23" flaps_full_deg="25">
  <source>Flight, 29 October 1936 and 17 and 24 December 1936</source>

  <!-- 40,500 lb, the standard boat: the model's 34,717 lb as it loads - its
       empty weight, 27,528 lb, the four crew it seats, its radio, oil, freight
       and 4,704 lb of petrol - and 5,783 lb more in the spar mail compartment,
       at the centre of gravity. -->
  <loading name="standard" total_lbs="40500">
    <pointmass index="5" lbs="5783"/>
  </loading>

  <!-- 45,000 lb, the long-range boats: as the standard boat, and 10,283 lb in
       the spar mail compartment. Their extra weight was petrol, in the wing;
       the model's wing tanks are at 9.21 m aft of the bow and the compartment
       at 9.3, and the model starts its tanks beyond the first two empty
       whatever a loading here sets them to. -->
  <loading name="long-range" total_lbs="45000">
    <pointmass index="5" lbs="10283"/>
  </loading>

  <figure name="water_takeoff_time_long_range" flight="water_takeoff" loading="long-range"
          unit="s" value="30.5" tolerance_percent="15" running_pitch_deg="8" rotate_kcas="60">
    Gouge, p. 649, of the tests at 45,000 lb: "30.5 seconds from full opening of
    the throttles, and a run of 795 yards"; the same in Flight, 10 December
    1936, p. 619.
  </figure>

  <figure name="water_takeoff_run_long_range" flight="water_takeoff" loading="long-range"
          unit="yd" value="795" tolerance_percent="15" running_pitch_deg="8" rotate_kcas="60">
    The same tests' run: 795 yards.
  </figure>

  <figure name="water_takeoff_time_standard" flight="water_takeoff" loading="standard"
          unit="s" min="21" max="27" running_pitch_deg="8" rotate_kcas="60">
    Gouge's Table II, p. 680, from flight tests: take-off time 24 s; the
    specification panel, p. 440f: 21 s. From the panel's 21 to an eighth over
    Gouge's 24.
  </figure>

  <figure name="draught" loading="standard" unit="ft" value="4.0" tolerance="0.5"
          keel_aft_ft="5.97" keel_below_ft="6.0">
    Scaled from the general arrangement's waterline, p. 440f; see above.
  </figure>

  <figure name="level_speed" loading="standard" unit="mph" value="200"
          tolerance_percent="5" altitude_ft="5500" boost_psi="2.5" propeller_lever="0">
    Specification panel, p. 440f: maximum speed 200 mph at 5,500 ft; Table II
    the same. Flown at the Pegasus Xc's normal maximum boost, +2 1/2 lb, the
    airscrews in coarse pitch.
  </figure>

  <figure name="climb_rate" loading="standard" unit="ft/min" value="950"
          tolerance_percent="10" speed_kcas="100" boost_psi="2.5" propeller_lever="1">
    Specification panel, p. 440f: rate of climb at sea level 950 ft/min;
    Table II, 933 ft/min. Flown at +2 1/2 lb, the airscrews in fine pitch, at
    100 knots, no climbing speed being given.
  </figure>

  <figure name="stall_speed_landing" flight="stall_speed" from="measured"
          loading="standard" unit="KCAS" value="66.32" tolerance="3"
          flaps_deg="25" entry_kcas="120" gear="1">
    **Measured, not published.** No flight manual for this aeroplane is
    public, so there is no handbook stall speed to hold the model to. This is
    what the model itself does, flown by the same deceleration the published
    stall speeds are measured by, on 2026-09-23. What it asserts is that the
    model still does what it did, not that the aeroplane does it. Measured
    from entry speeds of 120 and 160 knots it comes out the same to a
    hundredth of a knot, so the number is the model's and not the entry's.
    From 80 knots - only a fifth above the stall - it reads 69.2, because
    there is not enough speed in hand to establish the deceleration before
    the wing gives up; that is the entry being wrong, not the number.
  </figure>
</published_figures>
